    <DataNode elementId="AT5G23190-MONOMER" textLabel="AT5G23190-MONOMER" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9FMY1" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="415" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE, zhang">The Arabidopsis CYP86B1 is highly expressed in roots. Comparing to the wild type, the mutant plants had a different root and seed coat aliphatic polyester composition in which C22- and C24-hydroxyacids and their corresponding dicarboxylates were strongly reduced. It indicated that CYP86B1 encodes a very long chain fatty acid &amp;omega;-hydroxylase, which prefers the C22 and C24 substrates |CITS: [19525321]|. This substrate specificity differs from the only other fatty acid &amp;omega;-hydroxylase known to be involved in suberin biosynthesis in Arabidopsis, CYP86A1. The in vivo substrate, i.e. free fatty acid, CoA ester, or fatty acid bound to glycerol, of CYP86B1 is unknown. No enzyme activity and kinetic characterization data is available.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT5G23190-MONOMER" />
